[Music]  delighted to say we're joined now by  jeremy hunt he's the longest serving  health secretary in england he's now  chairs the select committee  and is a conservative back venture  jeremy welcome to the program  good afternoon let me ask you this  clearly the roll out of the vaccine is  both a  a vital medical imperative in dealing  with corvette  and it's very important as we've been  saying politically for mr johnson and  his  government what will be the challenges  what are the biggest challenges you  think  the government faces in getting this  role out to 13 million  of the most vulnerable before february  is out  well it is a massive logistical exercise  but we have one big advantage over the  problems that we had with  track and trace and the the contact  tracing and the centralized testing  laboratories last year  which is that we're not using new  infrastructure to do this we're using  our existing network of gps  supplemented by new centers that are  being set up but a lot of it will be the  existing  infrastructure and i'm actually quite  optimistic that the nhs will rise to the  challenge i think that  the numbers that have been done so far  are actually very impressive  to have got to more than a million  already and i think the  the challenge we've got is actually not  on the vaccine rollout it is the  question of whether the nhs is going to  fall over  because the health service journal is  saying that  within two weeks london will run out of  intensive care  capacity but we know from previous  lockdowns with this  virus there's a three-week delay between  doing something big like a lockdown  and it actually feeding through into  hospital admissions  and that is too long when it comes to  london  so um i think it's going to be a very  very nail-biting  next few weeks is is it too late  to do anything about that hospital  capacity now is it  inevitable that in the next couple of  weeks we'll get to where you described  okay  is there emergency action that can be  taken there is and i don't actually  think we will get to the point where uh  someone who needs an intensive care bed  doesn't get one because the nhs at the  moment is  will be scrabbling around frantically to  try and source those extra beds whether  from the private sector whether from  neighboring trusts and i think they'll  do it but what i don't think will happen  is that we will  i don't think we'll be able to avoid the  knock-on effects on things like  cancer care maybe some people  who might have been sent to intensive  care or borderline case  in previous times won't get sent this  time  who knows you know the outcome might not  be as good for that person so i'm afraid  the kind of knock-on effects that we saw  in the first  lockdown in terms of the impact on other  nhs care  will be very severe the government has  been widely criticized over the months  for its handling of the whole covert  crisis  every government's been criticized but  mr johnson's government has come in for  more than most is is the vaccine it's  get out of jail card  does that is it the does the vaccine  overwhelm all the other criticisms  i think it could i i'd rather agree with  james's analysis  just now um we were too slow to go into  lockdown  too slow to adopt uh test and trace as a  methodology  um but we have been the best in the  world  at approving vaccines and in terms of  distributing them just behind israel so  better than any other comparably sized  country and i think  we can say with a lot of pride it wasn't  just the brilliance of british science  and of course the fisa vaccine wasn't  developed in this country but the  obviously the oxford astrazeneca one i'm  going to put the word oxford in even if  nicola sturgeon won't  uh it was and i think that may turn out  to be the best vaccine  as it happens um but actually government  decisions  i mean matt hancock uh set up the  vaccine task force with kate bingham  you know that had controversy in the  early days he made a big  play gambling on pre-buying  quantities huge quantities of different  types of  vaccines and those gambles paid off  and that was a ministerial decision and  he should get a lot of credit for that  so  i think if this works out people will  say well we had a very tough pandemic we  didn't get it all right but we got some  things  very right and if we're the first  country to get back to normal  that will be a very very big fill up for  the government  i think most people regard this as a  national effort almost like a wartime  effort to rule out this  vaccine and it has to be all hands to  the deck that's  understandable so what do you make then  of when  we hear that public health england says  it won't work on sundays  i do think that's extraordinary i'm a  little bit hesitant  to to to pile in until i actually know  that's true  but if it is true and if i had been a  minister i would have been  sending that recommendation right back  and saying what on earth are you talking  about this is absolute nonsense  and has the government i mean it has  seemed that  time and again the government has been  behind the curve i think it's a  criticism  you have made of them too particularly  when it comes to lockdown  is that because of mr johnson's kind of  natural libertarian instincts that he's  always been reluctant to do  what he's ended up having to do as we've  seen this week  i think it may be that and in some ways  i think that because of those instincts  when we do finally do a lockdown there's  quite a lot of public support for it  because  people do recognize this is not  something the government has  rushed into and i think there are  genuine issues  i mean i think we should have gone in  earlier and actually even this week i  think we should have made the decision  last week because i'm not really sure  what data was  only available on monday that we  couldn't have seen on thursday or friday  of last week  but nonetheless uh he you know on monday  he had a meeting on monday afternoon he  made the decision we went  bang into lockdown from the following  morning that is a whole lot quicker than  the  speed that decisions were made at the  start of the pandemic  where are we equipped to deal with this  i mean clearly not when you look at  what's happened but  you were sexually safe for health when  this operation  cygnus the the pandemic war game uh  was carried out and it was a very  extensive  war game and i read quite a lot about it  but it wasn't  for covert type pandemic it was  it was the wrong war game wasn't it  that's exactly  the point i mean all the recommendations  that were made to ministers  following that pandemic were implemented  and there were some that were very  useful for this pandemic for example  drafting the emergency powers  legislation that was a pretty important  piece of work that came out which we  sadly did have to put into  practice but if you look through that  report  there is not a mention anywhere of  testing not once  and that kind of sums up that we were  not just in this country but across the  whole of europe and america  there was this group think that pandemic  equals flu pandemic and  you know looking back on it you have to  say  you know we had sars in 2002 mers in  2014  they happen to be in other parts of the  world but  i think it's very important that we set  up structures now  to make sure that that group think of  which i was part  doesn't take hold going forward well  let's go forward a little bit finally uh  jeremy hunt uh from what you've been  saying  it seems possible that if we get the 13  million roll out to the most vulnerable  groups  by the end of february we will after  that begin to see  the death rate and the case numbers  begin to fall we can then get more a lot  more than 13 million  and it may not be pie in the sky to  think  that sometime after easter which is very  early this  year in april there could be  not an event that's a return to  normality  but the beginnings of a process that  heads towards  some semblance of normality well i'm an  optimist and i've booked a family  holiday for the summer half term which  is the first week of june so um  i very much hope that uh i don't have to  get another of these bloody vouchers  that the airlines give you when you  cancel your  your tickets but i um i do think there  is a lot of risk  and i think we've just got to recognize  the most important  thing is something we're going to hear  in the next two weeks  which are the results of the test which  they're doing public health england are  doing important down  at the moment which say whether the  vaccine works  against the new kent strain  and then about a week after that we  should hear whether the vaccine works  against the south african strain  and that is the bit of information that  i am really  waiting for before i start banking  everything on easter  [Music]  you

